@test/1b | T | test -r, -w and -x answer for this process's access (a mode-000 file is unreadable to a user, an executable is executable), -L/-h for a link, -ef for two names of one file
! from the script census: `test -x` is false for an executable regular file, `-w` true for a read-only one, `-r` true for a mode-000 one, `-L`/`-h` false for a symbolic link, `-ef` false for two links to one file (XCU test). The most-counted gap: git's test-lib, ltmain.sh, debconf's confmodule, every init script, `service`, `which`.
--- test/c_s_test_access
# test -r/-w/-x ask whether THIS process may read, write or execute or
# search the file (XCU test), and -L/-h whether it is a symbolic link;
# -ef whether two names are one file (POSIX.1-2024).
: >plain
chmod 644 plain
cp /bin/sh prog 2>/dev/null || : >prog
chmod 755 prog
: >secret
chmod 000 secret
: >ro
chmod 444 ro
mkdir dir
ln -s plain link
ln plain hard
for f in plain prog secret ro dir link /usr/bin/env /nonexistent; do
    r=n w=n x=n l=n
    test -r "$f" && r=y
    test -w "$f" && w=y
    test -x "$f" && x=y
    test -L "$f" && l=y
    [ -h "$f" ] || l="$l-"
    echo "$f r=$r w=$w x=$x L=$l"
done
test plain -ef hard && echo "ef: same file"
test plain -ef prog || echo "ef: different files"
test -f /usr/bin/env && test -x /usr/bin/env && echo "env is a program"
chmod 600 secret
@umask/4 | T | umask sets the mask that files made by the shell and its children receive, and reports it in octal and with -S
! from the script census: `umask` is not implemented ("not supported yet", status 2) (XCU umask). install-sh, mkinstalldirs, config.guess, lesspipe and two Debian postinst scripts stop or misbehave on it.
--- umask/c_s_umask_set
# umask (XCU umask): set, report in octal and with -S, and reach the
# files the shell and its children create.
umask 027
umask
umask -S
: >f1
mkdir d1
(umask 077 && : >f2)
: >f3
ls -ld f1 d1 f2 f3 | cut -c1-10
umask u=rwx,g=rx,o=
umask
umask a+r
umask -S
@trap/17 | T | trap takes a condition as a signal number for every signal it takes by name (1 2 13 15, 14), and lists it by name
! from the script census: `trap action 13` (a signal number with no wolf meaning) is refused with "no such condition" and status 1 where dash and bash record it (XCU 2.15 trap, XSI numbers). `trap action PIPE` by name is accepted. Seen in config.guess, test-driver, tap-driver.sh, ylwrap, configure.
--- signal/c_s_trap_by_number
# `trap` takes a condition as a signal number as well as a name (XCU
# 2.15 trap; XSI for the numbers other than 0, and every script from
# automake's helpers to configure writes `trap … 1 2 13 15`). Setting
# an action for 13 (PIPE) or 14 (ALRM) is not an error, and `trap`
# lists it by name.
trap 'echo caught' 1 2 13 15
echo "set: $?"
trap '' 14
echo "ignore: $?"
trap >listed
sort listed
trap - 13 14
echo "reset: $?"
trap 'echo bye' 0
@2.14.1/8 | T | A bracket expression holding a slash matches a slash in a case pattern and in the four trim forms (the explicit-slash rule is for file names only)
! from the script census: `case` and ${x#pat}: a bracket expression that contains `/` matches a slash (XCU 2.14.1; the explicit-slash rule of 2.14.3 is for pathname expansion only). Stops every autoconf configure at its directory check; also ylwrap.
--- glob/c_s_bracket_slash_case
# A slash inside a bracket expression is an ordinary member in a `case`
# pattern and in ${x#pattern}: XCU 2.14.3's rule that a slash must be
# matched explicitly is for pathname expansion only (2.14.1, 2.6.2).
for w in /abs rel ./rel 'a\b' '$var/x' c:/dos; do
    case $w in
    [\\/$]* | ?:[\\/]*) printf '%s: absolute\n' "$w" ;;
    *[\\/]*) printf '%s: has a separator\n' "$w" ;;
    *) printf '%s: plain\n' "$w" ;;
    esac
done
case a/b in a[/]b) echo "bracket matches slash" ;; *) echo "no match" ;; esac
case a/b in a[!x]b) echo "negated bracket matches slash" ;; *) echo "no match" ;; esac
x=usr/local/bin
echo "${x#*[/]}" "${x%[/]*}" "${x##*[/]}" "${x%%[/]*}"
@2.13/12 | T | A pipeline stage inside a command substitution writes to a descriptor the substitution duplicated (exec 4>&1), not to the shell's own output
! from the script census: In `$( exec 4>&1; { echo x >&4; } | cmd >&3 )` the stage's write to descriptor 4 does not reach the substitution (it is empty) (XCU 2.7.5, 2.12). zdiff, zgrep and xzgrep read a decompressor's exit status this way and fail.
--- redir/c_s_cmdsub_fd_pipeline
# Inside a command substitution, `exec 4>&1` makes descriptor 4 the
# substitution's own output; a pipeline stage that writes to 4 writes
# there, while its standard output goes down the pipe (XCU 2.7.5,
# 2.12: a subshell inherits open files). The gzip family's wrappers
# carry an exit status out of a pipeline this way.
exec 3>&1
status=$(
    exec 4>&1
    (false 4>&-; echo $? >&4) 3>&- | cat >&3
)
echo "status=[$status]"
status=$(exec 4>&1; { echo seven >&4; } | cat >&3)
echo "status=[$status]"
status=$(exec 4>&1; echo data | { cat; echo eight >&4; } >&3)
echo "status=[$status]"
@2.5.2/16 | T | Unquoted $* on the right of an assignment or as a case word joins with the first character of IFS
! from the script census: Unquoted `$*` on the right of an assignment (and as a `case` word) joins with nothing instead of the first character of IFS (XCU 2.5.2). configure computes host_os as `linuxgnu`, so every `*-gnu*` host check takes its default arm (two checks answer "guessing no", two extra checks run).
--- param/c_s_star_assign_ifs
# Where no field splitting is done — the right side of an assignment,
# a `case` word — an unquoted $* joins the positional parameters with
# the first character of IFS, as "$*" does (XCU 2.5.2). autoconf's
# configure splits the host triplet with IFS=- and rebuilds the OS
# part with `host_os=$*`.
ac_save_IFS=$IFS
triplet=x86_64-pc-linux-gnu
IFS=-
set x $triplet
shift
cpu=$1 vendor=$2
shift; shift
os=$*
IFS=$ac_save_IFS
echo "[$cpu] [$vendor] [$os]"
IFS=:
set a b c
x="$*"
y=$*
IFS=' '
echo "[$x] [$y]"
IFS=,
set p q
case $* in p,q) echo "case word joined" ;; *) echo "case word not joined" ;; esac
@2.2/1 | T | Any byte but NUL is data: bytes above 0x7f pass unchanged to a program's arguments, file names, read, command substitution and here-documents
! from the script census: Every byte above 0x7f becomes `?` when a word is handed to a program as an argument or a file name, when `read` reads it, and in a here-document (XCU 2.2: only NUL is excluded). update-ca-certificates loses a certificate whose file name is not ASCII.
--- quote/c_s_high_bytes
# Bytes above 0x7f are data: in an argument to a program, in a file
# name, in what `read` reads, in a command substitution and in a
# here-document (XCU 2.2, 2.6.3, 2.7.4, read; a shell passes on any
# byte but NUL). The hex dumps are the proof; the script itself is
# ASCII.
e=$(printf '\303\251')
printf '%s' "$e" | od -An -tx1 | tr -d '[:space:]'; echo
/usr/bin/env printf '%s' "caf$e" | od -An -tx1 | tr -d '[:space:]'; echo
printf 'F\305\221t\n' | while read -r w; do printf '%s' "$w" | od -An -tx1 | tr -d '[:space:]'; echo; done
printf 'F\305\221t\n' | while read -r w; do /usr/bin/env printf '%s' "$w" | od -An -tx1 | tr -d '[:space:]'; echo; done
cat <<E | od -An -tx1 | tr -d '[:space:]'
x${e}y
E
echo
: >"n$e"
ls | od -An -tx1 | tr -d '[:space:]'; echo
for f in n*; do /usr/bin/env printf '%s' "$f" | od -An -tx1 | tr -d '[:space:]'; echo; done
l=$(printf 'a\377b')
/usr/bin/env printf '%s' "$l" | od -An -tx1 | tr -d '[:space:]'; echo
echo "${#e}"
@dot/8 | T | . reads any readable file, not only a regular one: /dev/null is an empty script, a FIFO is read
! from the script census: `. /dev/null` (and a FIFO, /dev/stdin) is "not found", which ends a non-interactive shell with status 2 (XCU 2.15 dot). pfetch does `. "${PF_SOURCE:-/dev/null}"` and prints nothing.
--- builtin/c_s_dot_special_file
# `.` reads commands from any readable file the name resolves to, not
# only a regular one (XCU 2.15 dot): /dev/null is an empty script.
. /dev/null
echo "dev null: $?"
mkfifo pipe
(echo 'echo "from the fifo"' >pipe &)
. ./pipe
echo "fifo: $?"
